Z||000004||Richard Parnell||05-19-2000||12:34 AM||||I've been using that clutch for a couple of years now. The clutch disengaged fine until yesterday when the master crapped out I pushed in the pedal and the spring in the master couldn't push the rod back out to push the pedal back because the seal had swollen so much.<BR>What's weird is I can go out there right now and put ATF in the system and it will work until the seals go bad.  Maybe some one knows what seals I can rebuild the master and slave with that ATF won't ruin.||||reg||1
Z||000005||William Federle||05-19-2000||01:45 PM||||I'm not an ace mechanic but I faced a similar situation not too long ago with my 1984 Fiero - so maybe something in this will help:<P>From the time I first started trying to bleed the clutch until I finally got the new stuff in and bled, I must have used at least 1/2 gallon of brake fluid. I had glasses on but I got some in my eye - that's how I learned to put the plastic over the slave cylinder when trying to bleed it.<P>1.    I replaced the master cylinder because I noticed that when my wife held in on the clutch while I watched the slave cyl. it crept back to the left.<P>2.     I replaced the aluminum clutch with a steel one, and had to find/modify a bushing to adapt it to the banjo of the new master cyl. I didn't put the spring on the clutch pedal shaft back in.<P>3.     I jacked the left rear of the car up about 6 or 8 inches.<P>4.     I had the clear hose going to a mason jar attached to the bleeder - I had the wife slowly push in on the clutch all the way to the floor and had her tell me when it hit the floor.<P>5.     When the clutch hit the floor I opened the bleed valve and got a lot of big bubbles. I never did quit getting the really tiny bubbles - now and then.<P>6.     I closed the valve and had the wife release the clutch.<P>7.     I repeated the bleed operation about 5 times, and in between each time, I topped off the master cylinder.<P>8.     I then removed the bleed valve and gave it to my wife along with the box end wrench that fit it. Then I put a piece of clean plastic over the hole the bleed valve came out of and standing in the trunk, I clamped a pair of vicegrips near the end of the slave cyl shaft and pushed it back into the cylinder and held it there while my wife threaded the valve back into the slave cyl. and tightened it.<P>     Now the slave doesn't go back to the left while you're holding in on the clutch and it releases the clutch. To get to reverse though, I have to shift into third and then into reverse.<P>I hope this helps.<P><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 05-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||05-19-2000||02:21 PM||||Have you tried gravity bleeding yet. It has worked every time for me. Run a search, it will come up if you don't know the procedure. Its a one man job and I am able to bleed my clutch in 10-15 minutes using it.||||reg||1
